WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.522
Use this final guide when you are unsure.

00:00:02.522 --> 00:00:05.792
Ask what the outer query needs from the inner query.

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:08.227
One value suggests a scalar.

00:00:08.227 --> 00:00:13.612
Membership suggests IN. A related-row question suggests EXISTS or NOT EXISTS.

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:17.382
A comparison against a set suggests ANY or ALL.

00:00:17.382 --> 00:00:18.732
Intermediate rows suggest a derived table.

00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:24.987
Then ask whether the inner query depends on the current outer row.

00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:27.062
If yes, it is correlated.

00:00:27.062 --> 00:00:32.657
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:37.987
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:44.837
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:49.677
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:54.918
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
